      {
        "id": 142591,
        "name": "cnranger",
        "username": "cnranger",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/c/53a042/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2015-06-18T18:55:47.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Silverleaf\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/silverleaf/48/2332_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Silverleaf:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Finally I'm not sure how a \"hero is forced to fall in love with villain\" would manifest itself (in game terms) as anything other than a denial effect, for example not being able to attack the villain. And denial effects are boring.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Affected hero takes psychic damage when villain is damaged, damage would probably come from directly from hero. (Pained to see villain hurt.)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Affected hero retaliates against any target that damages the villain. (Takes vengeance against those that harm the villain)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Some variation on Miss Information's Isolated Hero, where the hero can't affect or be affected by other heroes. (Doesn't want to help fellow heroes any more)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The first damage to the villain each turn is redirected to the affected hero. (Jumps in front of villain to defend)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>To be clear I agree with the rest of what you said, I just thought there were more ways to show \"fall in love with villain\" other than denial.</p>",
        "post_number": 25,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:19:51.993Z",
